n have very large impact  on our supply and demand and other  business metrics and so in order to  enhance the user experience we want to  have highly accurate estimates of the  impact of such special events now the  classical statistical approaches that  I've shown you on previous slides we  find them to be typically not very  amenable to such problems either because  you cannot incorporate external factors  at all or if you can they typically do  not perform well so we have heavily  invested in both machine learning as  well as deep learning methodologies and  here on this slide you can see an  example of one of the architectures that  we have used the first thing I want to  call out in the spirit of automation is  the pre-training step which includes an  encoder decoder approach which allows us  to automatically extract various  different features within the time  series we then concatenate the learned  embedding with various different events  data such as weather concerts holidays  and other types of events and then pass  it through a prediction network in this  case a multi-layer perceptron in order  to yield the final forecast in case  you're interested in more details we've  also published a white paper on this  topic and the link can be found at the  bottom of this slide we have also  ensured very high quality assurance on  this platform the first thing is of  course the data that is flowing into the  platform as well as of course ensuring  that we maintain high quality for any  forecasts that we're distributing to our  internal end-use  we've also deeply integrated the  forecasting platform into our data and  infrastructure stack in order to allow  for forecasts programmatically at scale  the second example of data science  commoditization I would like to go into  is natural language and conversational  AI and I would like to use as a concrete  example here one-click chat which is a  recent product feature that we have  launched in order to showcase some of  the common aspects of conversational AI  problems so here in one-click chat the  purpose is to help connect writers and  driver partners during the pickup  experience in particular to provide  suggested replies to the user based on  incoming messages that they're getting  and you can see an example of this on  the far right hand side so let's look at  one-click chat in the framework of  natural language problem there are  really two major steps here the first  one is given an incoming message for  example from a writer we want to extract  the intent or topic of conversation  now once the intent is found we then  retrieve various different replies that  match this intent and surface it to the  end user for example in this case to the  driver partner where they can choose to  pick one of those for example now this  seems like a very straightforward  problem but looking at it in practice  there various different challenges that  we're faced with firstly these messages  are very short they also have  abbreviations misspellings and also  autocorrect is not our friend as you can  see from this real-world example at the  bottom here I'm Washington for you this  should clearly mean I'm waiting for you  so any method that we want to use here  should not involve frequency based  methods such as tf-idf but rather should  be able to capture the semantic meaning  of the various different texts that  we're getting and so here we've used  Google stock to vaca methodology in  combination with nearest  Labour's approaches and trained it on  anonymized uber data and we were very  excited to see that the example of I'm  Washington for you is actually correctly  classified then as I'm waiting for you  so as mentioned earlier when we platform  eyes we're always looking for a wealth  of applications across the company to  help improve the user experiences and so  again I would like to showcase to you a  couple of the use cases that we have in  this space in addition to the one-click  chat example that I've just walked you  through we also have been doing an alpha  launch around hands-free dispatch this  allows now for driver partners to accept  and reject trips using voice and also  the voice equivalent to one-click chat  that will allow driver partners to  freeform speak their responses to  incoming writer messages on the  right-hand side you can see our customer  care use case here we have built an  assistant that helps our customer  service representative with various  different tasks now of course the  customer service representative will  always have the final say here but the  natural language system helps understand  what the intents are can identify what  the issue types are that people are  writing in about can make suggestions  about which actions the customer service  representative might want to take and  then also provides recommendations on  which response templates the agents  wants to start off from when writing a  response to the end user we've seen over  eight point eight percent reduction in  average handle time and of course this  has also improved the user experience as  a result here a very high-level overview  of the platform that we have been  starting to build on this front as  mentioned the majority of natural  language conversational AI use cases  would have as a first step  intent identification to the topic of  conversation and this is done by the  same  dick parser many conversational AI  systems will have a back and forth  component which means we want to keep  track of the state of the system and  this is done by the dialog manager and  then finally of course we want to  convert back the semantic representation  into human understandable form and that  is done by the language generator now  the third and final example of data  science commoditization and  democratization I want to talk to you  about is the most recent endeavor that  we have been working on and that I'm  personally very excited about and that  is semi automating or fully automating  exploratory data analytics at scale so  I'm sure many of you are faced with  questions that you can see here on the  left-hand side at uber we're spending  hundreds of human hours every week  answering questions like these or coming  up with actionable business insights  wouldn't it be amazing if you had a  system that could automatically scan  through the data and answer such  questions we have started to build such  a system and our proof of concept looks  extremely promising here but then we  wanted to take it a step further what if  you didn't even have to ask the question  the first place what if we had an  algorithm that could scan through the  data surface interesting insights to  business analysts to our business  stakeholders who can then combine these  insights with their business acumen to  make actionable business decisions and  again here we have built a proof of  concept and we've gotten really positive  reviews from our internal users who have  told us that we have been able to find  various different insights that they  would not have come up with themselves  so this would potentially give us an  opportunity to scale data analytics and  to be less dependent on the number of  human hours that we can spend on this or  the number of hypotheses that we can  come up with at the final stage of my  presentation I would like to walk you  through our strategy of commoditizing  data science because building platforms  is a multi quarter  two-year initiative and so it is really  important for us to set our data  scientist and platform builders up for  success here the approach that we have  been taking is of three phases in the  first phase we embed specialist data  scientists into various different areas  of the business this allows us to get  very interesting insights about the  opportunities the pain points and that  can have informed design decisions on  the platform it also allows us to solve  use cases that are of great interest to  the business and to user experience  right there and then and to build strong  relationships that are trusting in  nature with our stakeholders now of  course this is not a scalable approach  and so we quickly go to building  workflows or various different recipes  that the broader data science community  can leverage for example a Python  notebook that is easily discoverable  that people can build off from and then  the final step is of course to build  these automated platforms that I  mentioned that make anyone at uber into  a data scientist and we do this by  strategically choosing various different  use cases that help augment the platform  and reuse various different components  of it in case you're interested in  learning more details about the work  that I've presented today or about other  areas that we have been commoditizing  across the company please check out our  uber engineering blog post and with this  I would like to thank you very much for  your attention  [Applause]
 

